  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 105 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nCurrent conditions/Today:\n\nThe morning mesoscale banded precipitation has exited the region \nthis afternoon with much cooler northerly winds filtering in from \nCanada. Steep low level lapse rates are mixing with low level \nmoisture and a lobe of vorticity pivoting across the Northland. Day \ncloud phase RGB is showing some green hues in the cloud cover \nadvecting in from the north indicative of glaciation and some \nshowery activity. Surface temperatures across the north remain above \nfreezing but the cold air aloft could mix in some snowflakes as\nthese scattered showers continue south through this afternoon \nand evening. \n\nTonight:\n\nIsolated showery activity will continue through the early portions \nof the night across the Northland as a PVA couplet rotates through \nthe cold air advection aloft. Lake effect showers will also ramp\nup across the South Shore this evening as 850mb temps will be \naround -9C with the surface of Lake Superior still sitting at a\nwarm 9C. This will lead to lake induced instability of around \n200 J/kg supporting the activity. Thermal profiles keep the \nmoist layer out of the DGZ so not expecting large flakes but the\ninstability in play could certainly lead to isolated areas \nseeing heavier bursts of snow at times. One tricky part of this \nforecast package is the forecasted overnight lows. The NBM seems\nto be on the very low side of all available guidance with the \nSouth Shore projected to be around 25-28F. Meanwhile, some of \nthe other AI models are suggesting temps closer to 30-33F which \nwould still allow for snow accumulation but not nearly as \nefficient as what the NBM is suggesting. For now, we have opted \nto raise Min Ts closer to 29-31F, especially considering the \nadded cloud cover which should keep it slightly on the warmer \nside. \n\nThursday/Friday:\n\nLake effect showers will continue for the South Shore and start to \ntaper off on Friday as winds begin to shift towards out of the \nnorthwest and drier air starts to advect in. Warm ground \ntemperatures will help to mitigate the impacts of snow accumulation \nbut the prolonged period could yield and inch or two in the higher \nterrain of Ashland and Iron Counties. The surrounding areas will \nlikely stay with a dusting to half an inch. The remainder of the \nNorthland will remain on the quiet side of things with temperatures \nnoticeably cooler for Thursday and Friday with highs staying in the \n30s. \n\nWeekend-Mid Next Week:\n\nUpper level ridging will dominated the weather pattern through the \nweekend and early next week. This will keep weather uneventful and \nlead to a very gradual warming trend through the work week. \nTemperatures begin in the upper 30s and low 40s for Saturday \nand will climb into the upper 40s and low 50s by Wednesday. Not \nexpecting a pattern change until middle of the week when an \nupper level trough ejects out of the Four Corners which could \nlead to precipitation chances returning.",
